            <ArticleTitle>An &quot;acute abdomen&quot; in Antarctica. The problems of diagnosis and management.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>108-11</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Current Australian Antarctic activities require a unique medical service: a sole medical officer is responsible for the total care of 20 to 30 expeditioners (and of himself) for around nine months a year. A case of &quot;acute abdomen&quot; at Casey, Antarctica, is described and the problems of diagnosis and management, in this isolated medical practice, are discussed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
